er, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix ![](images/fa/fa51d7c585db408c47e8640ae839c1a3bbf94e2b0097e1ade511e5773461f1e8.svg) Taking the determinant, we have\n![](images/bc/bcf5c5dc9f5b7ed3849e719665f802040cf51e3334ae99e25ea7648cbb0f6134.svg)\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186121020 1064 "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations: \n\n  I. Swapping two rows\n\n  II. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\n  III. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix ![](images/fa/fa51d7c585db408c47e8640ae839c1a3bbf94e2b0097e1ade511e5773461f1e8.svg) Taking the determinant, we have\n![](images/bc/bcf5c5dc9f5b7ed3849e719665f802040cf51e3334ae99e25ea7648cbb0f6134.svg)\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 false] [17592186121061 1064 "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n![](images/fe/fe02091dde43a8a2405f6b588f619bc9fa3f54b31ac8a1ac39d318761f00cc3e.svg)\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186121061 1064 "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n![](images/fe/fe02091dde43a8a2405f6b588f619bc9fa3f54b31ac8a1ac39d318761f00cc3e.svg)\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." 13194139536395 17592186045417 false] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570726 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186124584 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570754 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186124584 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570755 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186124659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570780 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186124660 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570791 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570790 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570712 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570787 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570785 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570749 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570783 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570761 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186129243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570772 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186138444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570698 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186138798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186138799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570718 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570722 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570737 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570769 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570751 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570760 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570731 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570756 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570757 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186152374 1050 420.0 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186153426 1064 "The **negation** of a mathematical statement is its *opposite* statement. A mathematical statement and its negation have opposite truth values.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The negation of the *true* statement \"$4$ is divisible by $2$\" is the *false* statement \"$4$ is **not** divisible by $2.$\"\n- The negation of the *false* statement \"$5$ is even\" is the *true* statement \"$5$ is odd.\"\n\nWe can express negations using the symbol \"$\\neg$\". The negation of $A$ is denoted $\\neg A,$ and has the opposite truth values as $A{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nWe can negate compound statements as well. For example, the statement $\\neg (A \\land B)$ has the opposite truth values as the statement $A \\land B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 false] [17592186153426 1064 "The **negation** of a mathematical statement is its *opposite* statement. A mathematical statement and its negation have opposite truth values. \n\nFor example: \n\n- The negation of the *true* statement \"$4$ is divisible by $2$\" is the *false* statement \"$4$ is **not** divisible by $2.$\"\n\n- The negation of the *false* statement \"$5$ is even\" is the *true* statement \"$5$ is odd.\"\n\nWe can express negations using the symbol \"$\\neg$\". The negation of $A$ is denoted $\\neg A,$ and has the opposite truth values as $A{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nWe can negate compound statements as well. For example, the statement $\\neg (A \\land B)$ has the opposite truth values as the statement $A \\land B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\land B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186153427 1064 "When we have two negations in a row, we can cancel them out.\n\n- For example, $\\neg (\\neg A)$ can be simplified to $A.$\n\n- Likewise, $\\neg (\\neg (A \\land B))$ can be simplified to $A \\land B.$\n\nTo understand why negations cancel with each other, consider the following statement: \n\n$4$ is divisible by $2.$\n\nThe negation of this statement is \n\n$4$ is **not** divisible by $2.$\n\nIf we negate again, we get back the original sentence: \n\n$4$ divisible by $2.$\n\nSo, if we negate a statement twice, we end up with the original statement.\n\nMore formally, we can prove that $\\neg (\\neg A) \\equiv A$ using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg(\\neg A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nSince $A$ and $\\neg(\\neg A)$ have the same truth values, they are logically equivalent." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186153427 1064 "When we have two negations in a row, we can cancel them out.\n\n- For example, $\\neg (\\neg A)$ can be simplified to $A.$\n- Likewise, $\\neg (\\neg (A \\land B))$ can be simplified to $A \\land B.$\n\nTo understand why negations cancel with each other, consider the following statement:\n\n$4$ is divisible by $2.$\n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$4$ is **not** divisible by $2.$\n\nIf we negate again, we get back the original sentence:\n\n$4$ divisible by $2.$\n\nSo, if we negate a statement twice, we end up with the original statement.\n\nMore formally, we can prove that $\\neg (\\neg A) \\equiv A$ using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A\\,$ | $\\,\\neg(\\neg A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nSince $A$ and $\\neg(\\neg A)$ have the same truth values, they are logically equivalent." 13194139536395 17592186045417 false] [17592186162153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570732 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570753 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570700 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570701 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570721 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162155 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570723 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162155 1038 #edb/ref 17592186570730 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186162155 1038 #edb/ref 175