 "field-1" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570693 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570504 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570693 1079 #uuid "fae03ebe-384c-56a0-b28b-7e7a4779e500" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570158 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570248 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570258 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570367 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570515 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1077 "selection" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570258 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570694 1079 #uuid "fc9a310b-5a8f-5f99-b66d-f83152a549f6" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570123 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570265 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570456 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570507 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1075 #edb/ref 17592186570547 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1077 "selection" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1078 #edb/ref 17592186570456 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570695 1079 #uuid "ffea6c0c-7929-576d-9b9a-48a5f98cb7b3" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570588 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570696 1069 "q-7234" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570696 1071 "![](images/2c/2c1790cb306a749737594f994f6893d0146d5f2ed2458dc9e50ff9f4278475d7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({3}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570696 1072 #uuid "0355a074-20a2-5164-b4c8-0cc8e981bffa" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570696 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k({3}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={3}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570633 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570697 1069 "q-155959" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570697 1071 "Given that the line $y=x+2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570697 1072 #uuid "03601169-5838-5406-9812-fc2ccaab4423" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570697 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x+2k$ intersects the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}-x \\\\ y &=x+2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & =x+2k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=2,\\,b=-2,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4+16k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+16k & <0 \\\\ 16k & <-4 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ k & <-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570685 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570698 1069 "q-46125" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570698 1071 "A car travels at $1\\,000$ meters per minute. In kilometers, how far will the car travel in one hour?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570698 1072 #uuid "0ba5f5a5-9aa0-5b4c-ab5c-6490e52ab690" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570698 1074 "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{m}$ to $\\text{km}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{min}$ into $\\text{hour}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{1\\,000}$ kilometers per meter.\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}} & =1000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}} \\\\  & =1000\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\cdot 60(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}) \\\\  & =60\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, a speed of $1\\,000$ meters per minute is equivalent to $60$ kilometers per hour. Thus, the car will travel $60$ kilometers in one hour." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570589 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570699 1069 "q-72721" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570699 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(B)=-21.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{4}$ (switching the first and the fourth rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570699 1072 #uuid "0f13da88-9214-5888-9868-a4b7f8edacdd" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570699 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(-21) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570619 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570700 1069 "q-149649" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570700 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(2,5).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th></tr></tbody></table>" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570700 1072 #uuid "1200d290-e6bf-5cb3-acb4-bada082b8f96" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570700 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,5) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570606 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570701 1069 "q-149655" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570701 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(6,4).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570701 1072 #uuid "1222ccd8-a96c-520b-9c55-3b7bc8a21989" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570701 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570586 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570702 1069 "q-46832" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570702 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570702 1072 #uuid "13cd01f5-b70e-51c8-add8-d0b755916801" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570702 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})}{x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})(2+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4({2}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(4-x)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4(x-4)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570680 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570703 1069 "q-26883" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570703 1071 "Which of the following represents the graph of $y=6({5}^{x})?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570703 1072 #uuid "14efb3d8-c222-5e12-b8b9-d5a90aefed95" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570703 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}$:\n\n![](images/c0/c0bc33204b0cc8590f93529a76d4dd785fc52317505fcdee4a03022315da5333.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $6.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,6),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/11/11a8b1a124812c91be270a728ce07c3c2a9ea9f5b7bddd351ceb7d7dec7970f7.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570632 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570704 1069 "q-357229" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570704 1071 "The temperature of a cold room decreases by $\\frac{3}{8}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\frac{10}{3}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570704 1072 #uuid "1692e268-919b-5ead-b3e6-7be50cf4ba12" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570704 1074 "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{8}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{10}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{8}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})=-\\frac{10\\times 3}{3\\times 8}=-\\frac{10}{8}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{5}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570645 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570705 1069 "q-152280" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570705 1071 "The parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and the line $y=2x+1$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570705 1072 #uuid "1735602f-bd75-58b9-8473-899a4cca6ec8" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570705 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y &=2x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2{x}_{2}+1 \\\\  & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=3,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1+3=4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7751473a1f9c40086a3a765487cf24f8e5430150d015cd64f1ed0c5bd62fcee4.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570631 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570706 1069 "q-15814" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570706 1071 "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x-3|+3.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570706 1072 #uuid "17bccb94-8027-5ec1-b42e-ebe5775a61ec" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570706 1074 "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x-3|+3$ has $h=3$ and $k=3.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $3$ units *right* and $3$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/d5/d5c67a156d80c6dabd832539a4912c2bdd7ef38af9735fcade7ee46e4ba66eaa.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $3.$ This occurs at the point $(3,3).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570674 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570707 1069 "q-28074" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570707 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=-3(x+2)(x+1)(x-1)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570707 1072 #uuid "1d85a230-c47c-5ffa-b563-dedfdd86c871" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570707 1074 "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n-3(x+2)(x+1)(x-1)=-3x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=-3{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=1.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3(0+2)(0+1)(0-1) \\\\  & =-3(2)(1)(-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=6,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/47/472cf0dd7807c15e8e8334a163d1cfbfbbdcd974534e99b94222929048e72b31.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570663 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570708 1069 "q-112004" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570708 1071 "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-9x-18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570708 1072 #uuid "1fbfa8ab-6879-57fc-b2d0-196f095281bf" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570708 1074 "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & -9 & -18 \\\\  &  & -3 & 3 & 18 \\\\  & 1 & -1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x+2)(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+3)(0+2)(0-3) \\\\  & =(3)(2)(-3) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-18,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c3/c3d41c300747f4f0f18607351f2f03241c9a789ed70fe72e05c5a385b708c4c8.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570686 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570709 1069 "q-104295" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570709 1071 "Given $f(x)=\\frac{5-{x}^{4}}{6{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{‴}(x).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570709 1072 #uuid "224e49e4-5633-5d71-8ad0-69cc905bfd6e" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570709 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5-{x}^{4}}{6{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{-2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{-3}-\\frac{x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{-3}-\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{-4}-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{-4}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-20{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570642 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570710 1069 "q-144847" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570710 1071 "![](images/86/861e191e0f0b0d993e2d16f29da17ec9e258d657f6bdfc471e5fa9cf21a67b21.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(2)=4.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570710 1072 #uuid "25bfd26a-4014-54ec-bb25-41bebf6a0072" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570710 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/b7/b7f6e2f43ae474ec3194b2395144e8ea7dc9964da65bd018af08bb24b873bdd4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-1+5) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,-1,0\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570623 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570711 1069 "q-91377" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570711 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $7(x+8)^{3}=448.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570711 1072 #uuid "2640409b-d45b-5385-980a-142f0264d172" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570711 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+8)^{3}$ term and then taking the $3$rd root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+8)^{3}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x+8)^{3} & =448 \\\\ (x+8)^{3} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $3$rd root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+8)^{3} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(x+8)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x+8 & =\\sqrt[3]{{4}^{3}} \\\\ x+8 & =4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-4.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570583 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570712 1069 "q-82672" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570712 1071 "![](images/bf/bfc628070a42e0c26d135fd100233ae0d13bfdcb5f327d294e2201d8e58ca6ee.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $g(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570712 1072 #uuid "265ca0ae-198f-5493-91d7-17b0fcad6c85" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570712 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $g(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $g(5)=0$ and $g(9)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=5$ and $x=9.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570694 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570713 1069 "q-162918" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570713 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{36{t}^{2}}{25}}=$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570713 1072 #uuid "2a409742-71a6-54c6-bd19-7151d386a675" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570713 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{36{t}^{2}}{25}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{25}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(6t)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|6t|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|6|\\cdot |t|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\cdot |t|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{6|t|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570660 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570714 1069 "q-222360" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570714 1071 "The minimum value of the function $y=|x+3|+2$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570714 1072 #uuid "2b65f25b-10a0-5e3f-8752-11a285fdf596" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570714 1074 "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+3|+2$ has $h=-3$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $3$ units *left* and $2$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/81/81bace946b2fb425efa7fd7c54eb53ec353be5d9ef1516d9cf24e2c13656a60b.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $2.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570608 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570715 1069 "q-28365" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570715 1071 "Given that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-14x+8,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570715 1072 #uuid "2c01baff-ab5f-57ff-be23-ce994353bbde" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570715 1074 "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x),$ which means that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ So, to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & -14 & 8 \\\\  &  & -2 & 10 & -8 \\\\  & -1 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)(-{x}^{2}+5x-4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)({x}^{2}-5x+4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)(x-1)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the other roots of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-1)(0-4) \\\\  & =-(-2)(-1)(-4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $S$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=1,2,4$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=8,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/21/21dec37151f40778b8f094d3ae68cd8716c993535e53496cf7f148817c72e345.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570693 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570716 1069 "q-343087" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570716 1071 "Given $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3x,$ we have\n\n${f}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570716 1072 #uuid "30675b2a-1249-5c56-916f-1fdddcd8a518" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570716 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3x) \\\\  & =x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570625 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570664 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1069 "q-182367" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1072 #uuid "32972230-850f-5aff-94ba-0bb060b21910" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570717 1074 "The statement $A∧B$ is true if both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570611 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570718 1069 "q-176857" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570718 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n$0.75$ does **not** belong to the set of rational numbers.\n\nWhat is the negation of this statement?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570718 1072 #uuid "32db5c7a-ebc7-5981-a2ce-a4afccfedb03" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570718 1074 "Let's denote the given statement as $P.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\nIt is **not** true that $0.75$ does **not** belong to the set of rational numbers.\n\nIn other words, $0.75$ belongs to the set of rational numbers. Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$0.75$ belongs to the set of rational numbers.\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while $¬P$ is **true.**" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570665 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570668 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1069 "q-182374" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1072 #uuid "34fa5eb8-c611-5e47-9dfc-4ac699bfcccd" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570719 1074 "The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. \n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570659 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570720 1069 "q-338523" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570720 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570720 1072 #uuid "386a05f8-a2c4-54b1-8442-7cec1d9c62b6" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570720 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{3(1)+2}-\\sqrt{4(1)+1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1})(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{4x+1})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(3x+2)-(4x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-(1-x)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{1-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}[-(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})] \\\\  & =-(\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570675 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570721 1069 "q-162902" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570721 1071 "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=7$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $∗$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $?$ | $∗$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(Y=5)?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570721 1072 #uuid "38788558-45ed-5ac8-b24c-5c511e7f90ed" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570721 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,5) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=5) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=5) \\\\ 0.09 & =0.45\\cdot P(Y=5) \\\\ P(Y=5) & =\\frac{0.09}{0.45} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570638 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570722 1069 "q-176052" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570722 1071 "Given the statement\n\n$$\nP:2\\text{ divides }8\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents the statement $¬P?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570722 1072 #uuid "3d714d9e-2644-5dbb-a0ee-6c22b2b05b9a" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570722 1074 "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:\\text{It is not true that }2\\text{ divides }8,\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $2$ does *not* divide $8.$ Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:2\\text{ does }\\text{not}\\text{ divide }8.\n$$\n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570676 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570723 1069 "q-162826" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570723 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.38$ | $0.32$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.02$ | $0.28$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570723 1072 #uuid "3efec416-c341-5cd9-8d7e-9c91c7f8fc1f" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570723 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.32.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.6=0.42.\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=1)\\ne P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570610 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570724 1069 "q-102118" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570724 1071 "Express $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3})\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as an inequality." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570724 1072 #uuid "461aef2d-ef91-5bc9-a05c-59153c1a3f67" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570724 1074 "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](images/73/73f3c747217e98926510918c395eb644eb143ba07446a863b3f038aafb3fd19a.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3})$ can be expressed as the inequality $x<\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $(3,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x>3.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx<\\sqrt{3}\\text{ or }x>3.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570616 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570725 1069 "q-170784" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570725 1071 "Simplify the statement $(T∨T)∧(U∧U).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570725 1072 #uuid "4bc94003-3eae-5de3-85d1-2f1ad9dce7dc" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570725 1074 "Notice that:\n\n- the expression $T∨T$ simplifies to just $T,$ and\n\n- the expression $U∧U$ simplifies to just $U.$\n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to\n\n$$\nT∧U.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570590 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570726 1069 "q-132606" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570726 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570726 1072 #uuid "4cb48e08-10b0-5815-97ab-c688f0460cd6" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570726 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{(1/2)} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & ={f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2})(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4y-2 \\\\ z & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4y-1$ (or $4y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,4⟩.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570581 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570594 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570615 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570657 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1069 "q-182409" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧B)∧(A∨B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1072 #uuid "4d6cf595-2d25-5038-a5f2-0a891cc718e6" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570727 1074 "First, we complete a truth table for the first part in parentheses, $A∧B.$ The statement $A∧B$ is true if both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nNext, we append the truth table for the second part in parentheses, $A∨B.$ The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nFinally, we look at the column $(A∧B)$ and the column $(A∨B)$ to complete the column $(A∧B)∧(A∨B).$ The statement $(A∧B)∧(A∨B)$ is true if both $(A∧B)$ and $(A∨B)$ are true. \n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧B)∧(A∨B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570641 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570728 1069 "q-155535" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570728 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+3x-1$ and the line $y=-3x-10$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570728 1072 #uuid "569846cc-609e-5d75-9d24-71aff484c31e" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570728 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+3x-1 \\\\ y &=-3x-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =-3x-10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-1+3x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-10 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(-3,-1).$ Therefore, $p=-3,\\,q=-1,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=-3+(-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/91/91bedb41cd1b30cf392b23c0986e7b191ecf170fbdd4a0c34f5e2b8977c747b2.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570627 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570650 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570653 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570687 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1069 "q-212145" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,(B∧A)∨(B∨A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1072 #uuid "571dfb2b-950c-5c10-b8a7-797df910e9cf" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570729 1074 "First, we complete a truth table for the first part in parentheses, $B∧A.$ The statement $B∧A$ is true if both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B∧A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nNext, we append the truth table for the second part in parentheses, $B∨A.$ The statement $B∨A$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B∧A\\,$ | $\\,B∨A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nFinally, we look at the column $(B∧A)$ and the column $(B∨A)$ to complete the column $(B∧A)∨(B∨A).$ The statement $(B∧A)∨(B∨A)$ is true if either $(B∧A)$ is true, $(B∨A)$ is true, or both $(B∧A)$ and $(B∨A)$ are true.\n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B∧A\\,$ | $\\,B∨A\\,$ | $\\,(B∧A)∨(B∨A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570603 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570730 1069 "q-163615" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570730 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.07$ | $0.28$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.13$ | $0.52$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570730 1072 #uuid "5ad1bec0-7cb2-5524-90ce-c8dd7afd9873" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570730 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.07.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.35\\cdot 0.2=0.07.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.28=0.35\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.13=0.65\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.52=0.65\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570654 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570731 1069 "q-81368" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570731 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬[¬(¬B∨¬(¬A))].$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570731 1072 #uuid "5c770e58-f7f7-5bfc-9f03-f5a78e6b4cee" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570731 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So, we can simplify  $¬(¬A)$ to $A.$ \n\nAs a result of this, the statement simplifes to \n\n$$\n¬[¬(¬B∨A)]\n$$\n\nThen, we can simplify $¬(¬(¬B∨A))$ to $¬B∨A.$\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\n¬B∨A.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570688 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570732 1069 "q-143329" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570732 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570732 1072 #uuid "5dd8077d-1403-5bd2-b29d-34936c801dde" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570732 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.11.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.55\\cdot 0.2=0.11.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =0.44=0.55\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.09=0.45\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.36=0.45\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570584 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570733 1069 "q-152269" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570733 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=21-4x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}-{y}_{2}?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570733 1072 #uuid "5fa4d8a0-3fad-569b-a952-bc2efe6c446d" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570733 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2} \\\\ y &=21-4x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =21-4x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-21 & =0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-7,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(-7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,49).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{2} \\\\  & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,9).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=49,\\,{y}_{2}=9,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}=49-9=40.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8ca9ba76d66d80a0a564fbefd02342d3f63d99f6fbd907b736cdc9acee79b174.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570600 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570734 1069 "q-144875" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570734 1071 "![](images/cb/cba90791c239c986390a8a7fe1cb3ea0b4abb9cfd86ca13cba9ece6a5b4860b6.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,4]$ given that $f(4)=1.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570734 1072 #uuid "5fcb87aa-38a0-5213-a001-cd7978756f39" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570734 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/a8/a8bff8783c0e536cd0e3c475a8fca87a9861a1e1f0ca7f2833ca9c434da2426d.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-(-8) \\\\  & =1+8 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-(9+2-8) \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,9,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-5.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570591 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570735 1069 "q-130735" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570735 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(B)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(3B)?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570735 1072 #uuid "620b0d62-bee7-51dc-8bf7-49326cfad693" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570735 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=3$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(3B) & ={3}^{2}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570601 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570736 1069 "q-46837" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570736 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570736 1072 #uuid "662345c5-cd55-5265-8f67-e438e3ccf7b5" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570736 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3(3)}}{3-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{(3-\\sqrt{3x})(3+\\sqrt{3x})}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{{3}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x})}^{2}}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{9-3x}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3(x-3)}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570648 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570737 1069 "q-176853" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570737 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n$5$ belongs to the set of integer numbers.\n\nWhat is the negation of this statement?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570737 1072 #uuid "66c0453d-a98f-5149-84be-bb8778729997" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570737 1074 "Let's denote the given statement as $P.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\nIt is not true that $5$  belongs to the set of integer numbers.\n\nIn other words, $5$ does *not* belong to the set of integer numbers. Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$5$ does **not** belong to the set of integer numbers.\n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570656 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570738 1069 "q-72735" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570738 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=80.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{4}:=\\frac{3}{10}{R}_{4}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570738 1072 #uuid "687e18db-1843-51e7-a8a0-475f4a6d1676" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570738 1074 "The row operation applied to $B$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{3}{10}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{3}{10}det(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot 80 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570604 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570739 1069 "q-15811" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570739 1071 "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x+4|-1.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570739 1072 #uuid "6dff94b8-5d8c-56b5-9363-d9107e29b4d1" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570739 1074 "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+4|-1$ has $h=-4$ and $k=-1.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $4$ units *left* and $1$ unit *down*.\n\n![](images/45/45411bac1f347093b8b9931e1f8f8bee4f1eb411b6256a4c56d0ba33cffe603e.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-1).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570670 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570740 1069 "q-67654" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570740 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570740 1072 #uuid "6f0201d6-c736-51e8-8b5c-a5fa2133c2b7" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570740 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](images/52/52817b9c0ede04e8e920de8a52ebe025916f314cc937097c7fb91ea612e18f1b.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570667 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570741 1069 "q-15826" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570741 1071 "If $y=|x-5|$, what are the coordinates of the minimum point on the graph?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570741 1072 #uuid "7432d94a-3593-5858-b61c-052181b6a281" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570741 1074 "Remember that $y=|x-a|$ is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $a$ units right.\n\nThe function $y=|x-5|$ has $a=5.$ Therefore, it is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $5$ units right.\n\n![](images/ca/ca70d305bb0da32441611d879ca2a4a2453b38e951b448964b5d48f01fa33e28.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the coordinates of the minimum point are $(5,0).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570634 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570742 1069 "q-341826" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570742 1071 "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=4x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=0.$\n\n${f}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570742 1072 #uuid "75deedc5-439f-5c0b-9ed9-ea7f29313f69" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570742 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =4-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =4-{sec}^{2}⁡(0) \\\\  & =4-{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-{(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2}=4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $3.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570592 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570658 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1069 "q-250171" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1071 "Given that the line $y=x+3k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1072 #uuid "768c8258-584d-53be-9ed2-7212820baebf" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570743 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x+3k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y &=x+3k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =x+3k \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4(1)(-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-16 \\\\ k & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-2.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570695 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570744 1069 "q-28931" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570744 1071 "What's the domain of the function $f(x)={10}^{x}?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570744 1072 #uuid "7782f625-0fd2-5a62-8065-db81e75a2462" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570744 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={10}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/37/378d8bc3c7dadc1915e4db903147793b37fcdb4e1ed74481e14ca3f2ca7f1e18.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570597 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570745 1069 "q-338468" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570745 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570745 1072 #uuid "782d71a6-13dd-5aa9-bf77-a0e9f83d71b5" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570745 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570618 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570644 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570692 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1069 "q-182365" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1072 #uuid "7a8f6445-71b5-5585-b787-6ed5dc133a8a" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570746 1074 "The statement $A∧B$ is true if both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570640 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570747 1069 "q-30987" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570747 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+8.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570747 1072 #uuid "7c894a69-28f5-558a-ade7-8f7e7954ebf4" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570747 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570637 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570748 1069 "q-170789" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570748 1071 "Simplify the statement $R∧(Q∨(Q∧Q)).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570748 1072 #uuid "7ea98094-8950-5b20-861d-41decac9476c" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570748 1074 "First, the expression $Q∧Q$ simplifies to just $Q.$ \n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nR∧(Q∨Q).\n$$\n\nThen, the expression $Q∨Q$ simplifies to just $Q.$\n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nR∧Q.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570662 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570749 1069 "q-39499" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570749 1071 "![](images/ad/ad5f193a1ff21e03659789473a6e4918b6dd7ba25cd9db4e6fc0f729d0481391.png)\n\nWhat are the equations of the horizontal asymptotes of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570749 1072 #uuid "822263d6-7f5d-5a37-a89e-f93c0b6eb7bf" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570749 1074 "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-3.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -3\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a horizontal asymptote at $y=-3.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ also has a horizontal asymptote at $y=2.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570691 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570750 1069 "q-47984" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570750 1071 "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570750 1072 #uuid "83b5db67-0cfd-59f0-8254-1d32998685fc" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570750 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570677 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570751 1069 "q-176866" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570751 1071 "$9$ is divisible by $4$, or $8$ is **not** divisible by $3.$\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P:9\\text{ is divisible by }4$\n\n- $Q:8\\text{ is divisible by }3$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570751 1072 #uuid "846f1209-a84b-5c13-a0c8-7374d18fc658" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570751 1074 "The sentence\n\n$9$ is divisible by $4$ \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $P.$ \n\nThe sentence\n\n$8$ is **not** divisible by $3$\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n$9$ is divisible by $4$, or $8$ is **not** divisible by $3$\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $P∨¬Q.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570605 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570607 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570624 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570639 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1069 "q-212113" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧(B∨A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1072 #uuid "85a2c67b-54e6-59db-b93d-20860ae6f9ac" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570752 1074 "First, we complete a truth table for the part in parentheses, $B∨A.$  The statement $B∨A$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B∨A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nThen, we look at the columns labeled $A$ and $B∨A$ to complete the column $A∧(B∨A).$ The statement $A∧(B∨A)$ is true if both $A$ and $B∨A$ are true.\n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B∨A\\,$ | $\\,A∧(B∨A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570673 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570753 1069 "q-143436" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570753 1071 "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570753 1072 #uuid "86447238-489f-5596-b009-12655e2594dd" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570753 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.1.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.2\\cdot 0.8=0.16.\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=3,Y=2)\\ne P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570595 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570754 1069 "q-106283" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570754 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-2(x+13)^{2}+14?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570754 1072 #uuid "87522f6e-547f-5501-803f-38e69a4ab8bc" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570754 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-13$ and $k=14,$ the vertex is $(-13,14).$\n\n- Since $a=-2<0,$ the parabola opens downwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens downwards, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output. \n\nTherefore, the range is $(-\\infty ,14].$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/08/08b2ebf2fb3ecb8e02eed81d47e349dad22bde0d97e8920fabc75a8e2301da2b.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570684 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570755 1069 "q-90979" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570755 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-3(x+1)^{2}-7?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570755 1072 #uuid "8a71e9cb-8bb6-5c17-8cab-a2b0b8515a29" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570755 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-1$ and $k=-7,$ the vertex is $(-1,-7).$\n\n- Since $a=-3<0,$ the parabola opens downwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens downwards, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output. \n\nTherefore, the range is $(-\\infty ,-7].$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21049fc3aa8d6e2b64c4fd5db723c00e5878473ad8b96f25271c46edc48ed3ea.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570580 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570756 1069 "q-176871" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570756 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬P)∨¬(¬(P∧Q)).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570756 1072 #uuid "91ef8c77-f388-589e-b268-cf2103e707d0" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570756 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So: \n\n- we can simplify $¬(¬P)$ to $P,$ and\n\n- we can also simplify $¬(¬(P∧Q))$ to $P∧Q.$\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nP∨(P∧Q).\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570683 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570757 1069 "q-81357" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570757 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬A)∧¬(¬B).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570757 1072 #uuid "928cf87e-e0b7-5063-acab-2224d29c8984" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570757 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So:\n\n- we can simplify $¬(¬A)$ to $A,$ and\n\n- we can also simplify  $¬(¬B)$ to $B.$\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570587 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570758 1069 "q-30988" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570758 1071 "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-11.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570758 1072 #uuid "94bfc6e6-a43f-5249-b046-4e95dd242cc6" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570758 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570689 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570759 1069 "q-170781" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570759 1071 "Simplify the statement $(Q∨R)∧(P∨P).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570759 1072 #uuid "974d976d-65a4-5d23-86e7-77e1c81632fd" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570759 1074 "The expression $P∨P$ simplifies to just $P.$\n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to\n\n$$\n(Q∨R)∧P.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570622 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570760 1069 "q-81399" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570760 1071 "$9$ is **not** both even and prime.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P:9\\text{ is even}$\n\n- $Q:9\\text{ is prime}$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570760 1072 #uuid "97de13ba-d851-57a8-a13f-4fcc5cdc4d81" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570760 1074 "The given sentence is the negation of the following sentence: \n\n$9$ is both even and prime,  \n\nwhich can be expressed symbolically as $P∧Q.$\n\nThe negation is represented by the symbol \"$¬$\". \n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as $¬(P∧Q).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570655 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570761 1069 "q-47463" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570761 1071 "![](images/74/7471044234ec20534f33abb45d26db3c0a2876bed0fcc73844d1867b354491c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the graph shown above?\n\nI. $f(x)\\to 2$ as $x\\to -\\infty$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to 1$ as $x\\to \\infty$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\infty$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570761 1072 #uuid "994cad67-228c-54a3-8cec-2145c453420a" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570761 1074 "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\n- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570636 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570762 1069 "q-341876" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570762 1071 "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=6x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570762 1072 #uuid "9b9f5ad2-fc37-5cb1-8895-57f56e806362" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570762 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)=6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6-{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})})}^{2} \\\\  & =6-{(\\frac{1}{1/2})}^{2} \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $2.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570630 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570763 1069 "q-130769" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570763 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=11.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{4}:=3{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570763 1072 #uuid "a1f0bc2a-d097-538a-b002-360896d3c91f" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570763 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}:=3{R}_{4}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $3.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3det(A) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570621 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570764 1069 "q-170790" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570764 1071 "Simplify the statement $A∨(B∧(B∨B)).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570764 1072 #uuid "a5dc4674-bf70-593d-b79c-9ae4e2518f41" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570764 1074 "First, the expression $B∨B$ simplifies to just $B.$\n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to\n\n$$\nA∨(B∧B).\n$$\n\nThen, the expression $B∧B$ simplifies to just $B.$\n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to\n\n$$\nA∨B.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570682 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570765 1069 "q-75543" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570765 1071 "![](images/cc/cc2ea14272a30271bb022e9deb5e8b506ed0c2725d6633e7308b3786cb09d84c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570765 1072 #uuid "a9e5a7d0-a42d-53f5-8ae1-26c9c5fee794" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570765 1074 "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570620 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1069 "q-21134" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1071 "![](images/f6/f6408a8a100f18484b72e6a21243111c4445240a93f84068486e03290a2f8892.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1072 #uuid "ac232906-fa2f-5902-9534-48663339125f" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1074 "The element shown in the diagram is the part of the circle that is enclosed between the points $B$ and $D$ on the circle and passes through the point $C$ on the circle. So, this element represents the arc $\\overset{⌢}{DCB}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570628 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570767 1069 "q-338954" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570767 1071 "![](images/8c/8c0a344d98dc0ca3a6e4c0fdc3c98074bc2de23301300e663a02ea6f4dff3035.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,6}f(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570767 1072 #uuid "ae5c5c63-a42c-5945-a9b3-a559880f74b2" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570767 1074 "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $6$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570672 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570768 1069 "q-51034" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570768 1071 "![](images/84/84490ed5dee30eb93ae322d0d4ca20109672a80083bf9eabb252ae49c7501036.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570768 1072 #uuid "b2b0f174-efe7-5216-b895-6e427b351bde" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570768 1074 "As $x$ approaches $5$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty$, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is not defined for $x>5,$ and consequently, we cannot approach $x=5$ from the right. So we have \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n  \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570629 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570769 1069 "q-176050" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570769 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n$5$ is **not** prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of this statement?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570769 1072 #uuid "b52e0c2d-bb10-5ec6-8bde-b55f004808c0" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570769 1074 "Let's denote the given statement as $P.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\nIt is **not** true that $5$ is **not** prime.\n\nIn other words, $5$ is prime. Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$5$ is prime.\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while $¬P$ is **true.**" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570598 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570602 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570626 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570646 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1069 "q-182384" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧B)∨A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1072 #uuid "b60fbccd-7b1a-5212-9b58-db4841049d45" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570770 1074 "First, we complete a truth table for the part in parentheses, $A∧B.$  The statement $A∧B$ is true if both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nThen, we look at the columns labeled $A∧B$ and $A$ to complete the column $(A∧B)∨A.$ The statement $(A∧B)∨A$ is true if either $A∧B$ is true, $A$ is true, or both $A∧B$ and $A$ are true. \n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧B)∨A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570690 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570771 1069 "q-35500" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570771 1071 "![](images/d1/d1e5821252dd0634c6033a35247af033bcda8a96fa3733b65be52565a60ea3a6.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\,-2}f(x).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570771 1072 #uuid "b8283679-623f-5e9d-b3dc-5a2b30321fdc" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570771 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty$, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570582 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570772 1069 "q-39517" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570772 1071 "![](images/8f/8f7dead46db0b268a9474ad842d8d6077cc84971895e005a3686ab5b18131b6c.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty .$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570772 1072 #uuid "ba3e1985-7070-5883-9f3a-6c38d6b3795b" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570772 1074 "On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570651 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570773 1069 "q-78063" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570773 1071 "If $y=|x+1|$, what are the coordinates of the minimum point on the graph?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570773 1072 #uuid "bc3a1a0d-0a47-5924-a45a-6ae72b37f737" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570773 1074 "Remember that $y=|x-a|$ is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $a$ units right.\n\nThe function $y=|x+1|$ has $a=-1.$ Therefore, it is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $1$ unit left.\n\n![](images/38/38532ccdd10c7a60f26fdf35c837376af799d7b024370e8b223cd61b7b18eb9d.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the coordinates of the minimum point are $(-1,0).$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570678 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570774 1069 "q-44497" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570774 1071 "For what value of $k$ are the lines $3x+4y=8$ and $kx+4y=-20$ parallel?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570774 1072 #uuid "cb6eb9c0-dc4b-52b4-82f6-8cb252573f49" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570774 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $3x+4y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4y & =8 \\\\ 4y & =-3x+8 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx+4y=-20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+4y & =-20 \\\\ 4y & =-kx-20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{4}x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\frac{k}{4}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $-\\frac{k}{4}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{4} & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570612 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570775 1069 "q-163603" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570775 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570775 1072 #uuid "d05ba4df-d0b9-5ab9-9679-a4617e87b3ef" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570775 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=2)=f(0,2)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.6\\cdot 0.25=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,4) & =0.45=0.6\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.1=0.4\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.3=0.4\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570643 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570776 1069 "q-43045" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570776 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=3$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570776 1072 #uuid "d7915d65-a649-5e77-8661-8aa533c4bd16" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570776 1074 "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $1$st and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570617 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570777 1069 "q-287666" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570777 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(p-1)x=5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570777 1072 #uuid "db12ae2f-b0be-5f71-b38b-bd9f3742126a" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570777 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2(p-1),\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-1)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-1)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-1 & =0 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570649 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570778 1069 "q-344165" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570778 1071 "![](images/73/731237ee8d4ea5fdb53c50971bad11dd9f4317f595ae9af95846b17b6d064fce.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7.$\n\nGiven that $g(3)=4,$ what is the minimum value ${g}_{\\text{min}}$ of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]?$\n\n${g}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570778 1072 #uuid "df0b416e-f70a-5924-a6c6-f62bc6887f2f" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570778 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & =4 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-7 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-5.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570671 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570779 1069 "q-163167" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570779 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570779 1072 #uuid "e322b76b-ff51-56ef-b559-1a8882734867" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570779 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.3.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.6\\cdot 0.5=0.3.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =0.15\\ne 0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570609 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570780 1069 "q-90968" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570780 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+6x-8?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570780 1072 #uuid "e645c316-71e4-58c2-8dda-54dfd003d3ee" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570780 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0,$ $b=6,$ and $c=-8.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{6}{2\\cdot (-1)}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=3$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =-(3)^{2}+6(3)-8 \\\\  & =-9+18-8 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(3,1),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1].$\n\n![](images/36/362626b55b357f91efb0fc89a51361bd8a9be368a6645becd3db07580accaaff.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570679 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570781 1069 "q-3531" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570781 1071 "Factor $81{n}^{3}+24.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570781 1072 #uuid "e66f06d6-a158-572d-9c8e-dbcf45eb29af" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570781 1074 "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $3.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n81{n}^{3}+24=3(27{n}^{3}+8)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a sum of cubes:\n\n$$\n3(27{n}^{3}+8)=3((3n)^{3}+{2}^{3})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression in parentheses using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{n}^{3}+24 & =3((3n)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3(3n+2)((3n)^{2}-3n\\cdot 2+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(3n+2)(9{n}^{2}-6n+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570613 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570782 1069 "q-53020" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570782 1071 "![](images/d4/d4c1c22e521c157ee7e8d00e4ca545b98d04f56721c58c0b6ec46c2126e4f2ff.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a regular nonagon and a regular hexagon. Find the measure of the shaded angle." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570782 1072 #uuid "e8619f65-a151-5133-8a41-d7413e4c5c11" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570782 1074 "The shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular nonagon and a regular hexagon.\n\n![](images/a6/a64e0e57966f7b99b6d0f0af843dff25b7a87ed77347643212e04b0f5b8f5cd7.png)\n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum to ${360}^{∘}.$ In a regular nonagon, there are $9$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{9}={40}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a regular hexagon, there are $6$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{6}={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given angle has the following measure:\n\n$$\n{40}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={100}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570652 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570783 1069 "q-75999" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570783 1071 "![](images/c1/c12deadd41a9c2853d0d2d50ba5c19ac6ca13012d7672941c92fa5e6cc5df642.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true for the graph shown above?\n\nI. $y=-1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nII. $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570783 1072 #uuid "eab5bcd3-8a73-5188-a5f4-a81be3ecb0f7" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570783 1074 "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty\n$$\n\nThis means that $y=1$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x).$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis means that $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x).$ Therefore, III is also true.\n\n- The horizontal asymptotes of $f(x)$ are $y=1$ and $y=2.$ The line $y=-1$ is not a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ because the function does not level off to $-1$ on the right nor on the left. Therefore, I is false.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570647 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570784 1069 "q-105266" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570784 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=7x-4{x}^{2}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570784 1072 #uuid "f67b31bd-a1c3-55b5-a439-944a03b0aa36" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570784 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7x-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =7-2\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =7-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-8x) \\\\  & =0-8 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570596 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570785 1069 "q-51499" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570785 1071 "What is the root of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{5}-3?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570785 1072 #uuid "f70dd11d-74b0-578f-b3fa-cdba24eab4d5" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570785 1074 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{5}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{5} & =3 \\\\ 2x & =15 \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{15}{2}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570635 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570666 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1069 "q-212139" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1072 #uuid "f7b6faef-d0da-5b75-99b8-9ae3ba1031b8" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570786 1074 "The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. \n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570661 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570787 1069 "q-82676" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570787 1071 "![](images/64/642d1393aa1e2d0c6303cafcf576eb2bcd007995d17beca8c65a740e3a61f125.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570787 1072 #uuid "f97aefa8-bd86-5ea7-af68-778cc557d3b1" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570787 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that there is no value of $x$ that gives an output of zero. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has no roots." 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570599 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570788 1069 "q-37670" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570788 1071 "Factor the expression $\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{6}}{125}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570788 1072 #uuid "f995ab76-e781-54a1-b26b-a28e8f4d92d3" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570788 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{6}}{125}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{3}-{(\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5})}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{6}}{125} & ={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{3}-{(\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5})({(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}+{(\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5})(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{x{z}^{2}}{10}+\\frac{{z}^{4}}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570681 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570789 1069 "q-72746" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570789 1071 "Suppose $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(C)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(-4C)?$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570789 1072 #uuid "f9fbd8ca-7a83-50db-a6d3-a36d5d6ceb97" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570789 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-4C) & =(-4)^{3}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =(-64)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =320.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570669 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570790 1069 "q-82671" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570790 1071 "Which of the following tables describes a function that has $-5$ and $5$ as roots?" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570790 1072 #uuid "fadbbd96-8a4c-5964-b393-9b0639ffb5cd" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570790 1074 "The numbers $-5$ and $5$ are roots of $g(x)$ if $g(-5)=0$ and $g(5)=0.$  Of the given tables, only the following table has this property:\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $5$ | $2$ | $0$ |" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570585 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570593 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186570614 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1069 "q-250655" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-(x+7)(x+9).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1072 #uuid "fb786a48-6efc-557e-bdac-ba3e5bfceb11" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570791 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-9$ and ${x}_{2}=-7,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-9-7}{2}=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-8$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(x+7)(x+9) \\\\  & =-(-8+7)(-8+9) \\\\  & =-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-8,1),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 1.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cb245de80be7125a1e8874781ed21fc2845f25f7ee8e1e43356819f87fe1b6c.png)" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570792 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570854 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570792 1062 #uuid "025b43f4-b967-5253-bd0e-79c0a7e61eb7" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570792 1063 #edb/ref 17592186269236 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570793 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570815 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570793 1062 #uuid "05d1b5f7-911e-5855-8309-87882fbd1201" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570793 1063 #edb/ref 17592186570749 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570794 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570816 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570794 1062 #uuid "083d0182-6210-5520-86dd-1a05fdb0f8a2" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570794 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120204 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570795 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570869 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570795 1062 #uuid "0911f56d-289d-5081-87d9-624508a1e2bb" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570795 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125109 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570796 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570865 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186570796 1062 #uuid "0a11be39-e15b-5695-a209-6aa5aa9d56b2" 13194139536395 17592186